# yaml-language-server: $schema=../schemas/workflow.schema.json

name: Zero Problems
description: >-
  Zero out all errors in the VS Code Problems panel (get_errors). Triages
  every error by file location, fixes workspace files, and asks the user
  what to do with external files (delete, ignore, or move into project).

  Auto-iterates up to 3 fix-check cycles per file. If errors persist after
  3 cycles, escalates to the user with a detailed report.

  Worker delegation is tiered by complexity (bounded runSubagent swarm agents):
  - swarm-tiny-implementation: simple type annotations, missing imports, trivial fixes
  - swarm-standard-implementation: multi-line logic changes, refactors, complex type issues
  - swarm-senior-implementation: architectural problems, cross-file dependencies, design flaws

  Commits per-file after each successful fix.

  Composable: can be invoked standalone via `/run-workflow zero-problems`
  or referenced as a quality gate step in other workflows' postflight.

preflight:
  - prompt: >-
      Call get_errors (with no file filter) to capture the COMPLETE list of
      errors from the VS Code Problems panel. Parse the output into a
      structured inventory:

      For each file with errors, record:
        (a) absolute file path
        (b) whether it is INSIDE the workspace or EXTERNAL (outside workspace root)
        (c) number of errors
        (d) the full text of each error (line number, code snippet, error message)

      If the problems panel is already empty (zero errors), report
      "Zero problems -- nothing to fix" and STOP. Skip all remaining steps.

  - prompt: >-
      Triage the error inventory into two buckets:

      1. WORKSPACE FILES -- files inside the project workspace. These will be
         fixed in the main steps.

      2. EXTERNAL FILES -- files outside the workspace (system temp, other
         projects, or generated artifacts). For EACH external file, ask the user:
         "File <path> is outside the workspace and has N error(s). Action?"
           - Delete: remove the file
           - Ignore: skip it, leave as-is
           - Move: move it into the workspace (ask user where)

      Execute the user's chosen action for each external file immediately.
      After resolving all external files, the remaining work list should
      contain only workspace files.

steps:
  - prompt: >-
      For each workspace file in the triage list, assess the complexity of
      its errors and assign a worker:

      - SIMPLE errors (missing type annotations, unused imports, trivial
        syntax issues, single-line fixes) -- delegate to
        swarm-tiny-implementation

      - COMPLEX errors (multi-line logic changes, type system refactors,
        function signature changes, parameter type mismatches) -- delegate
        to swarm-standard-implementation

      - ARCHITECTURAL errors (cross-file dependency issues, design pattern
        violations, module-level restructuring needed) -- delegate to
        swarm-senior-implementation

      Build a fix plan: for each file, list the assigned swarm agent, the
      errors to fix, and the approach. Present the plan before executing.
      Before dispatch, run the swarm dispatch-preflight gate (resolve the
      capability tier vs available model strength; escalate if a required
      senior tier is unavailable rather than fixing architecture on an
      under-powered model).

  - prompt: >-
      Execute the fix plan. For each file, in order:

      1. Delegate the fix to the assigned worker via runSubagent. Include in
         the handoff: the file path, the exact error text(s), the surrounding
         code context, and clear acceptance criteria ("the error must not
         appear in get_errors after the fix").

      2. After the worker returns, call get_errors on that specific file to
         verify the fix resolved the error(s).

      3. If errors remain on that file, re-delegate to the SAME worker with
         the updated error text (iteration 2 of 3). Repeat up to 3 total
         attempts per file.

      4. If errors STILL remain after 3 iterations, escalate to the user:
         "File <path> still has N error(s) after 3 fix attempts. Errors:
         <details>. Manual intervention needed."

      5. If the fix is clean (zero errors on that file), commit the change:
         `holly task exec_in_current_task_worktree -- git add <file> && holly task exec_in_current_task_worktree -- git commit -m "fix(<scope>): resolve <error-type> in <filename>"`

      Track progress with manage_todo_list -- one todo item per file.

postflight:
  - prompt: >-
      Run get_errors one final time with no file filter to capture the
      complete state of the Problems panel.

      Report a summary:
        - Files fixed: N
        - Files escalated: N (with details)
        - External files resolved: N (deleted/ignored/moved)
        - Remaining errors: N

      If remaining errors is zero, print "Zero problems achieved."
      If remaining errors is non-zero, list them clearly for the user.
    category: quality

hooks:
  afterEach:
    prompt: >-
      After each main step, read the current error count by calling
      get_errors. If new errors have been INTRODUCED by a fix (error
      count increased), flag this immediately and include the new errors
      in the next step's context.
    category: quality
